WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:05.259
What’s up, Wisecrack?

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:08.639
Jared, again.

00:00:08.639 --> 00:00:13.169
We saw your requests, so today, we’re puttin’
the crazy hat back on to dive into the philosophy

00:00:13.169 --> 00:00:18.250
of Grant Morrison, writer of such acclaimed
works as All-Star Superman, Flex Mentallo,

00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:19.520
and Multiversity.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:24.270
Like Alan Moore, Grant Morrison was part of
the British Invasion that revolutionized comics

00:00:24.270 --> 00:00:25.270
in the ‘80s.

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:30.210
And also like Moore, he’s a self-proclaimed
magician with an affinity for psychotropic

00:00:30.210 --> 00:00:31.210
drugs.

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:37.370
“To me it's just a very simple equation
is that life plus significance equals magic.”

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:41.551
But there are major differences between them,
beyond their obvious discrepancy in their

00:00:41.551 --> 00:00:43.030
haircut philosophy.

00:00:43.030 --> 00:00:50.550
As insane as Moore seems, Morrison might be
even crazier.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:55.280
He wrote some fictional girlfriends for a
fictional version of himself in The Invisibles,

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:57.760
and then later claimed they appeared in real
life.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:58.760
“So, as a result of all this, I’d just
split up with my girlfriend.

00:00:58.760 --> 00:01:04.449
And I was like: ‘okay, I want a new one,
and I want her to look exactly like this chick

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:06.759
in the comic, ‘cuz she’s cool.’

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:11.740
So, I did a sigil; a month later, the girl
turns up.

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:12.740
Then, another one!”

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:16.829
And when he wrote the about this fictional
Grant Morrison being attacked by bacterial

00:01:16.829 --> 00:01:22.009
gods, a demonic attack and/or Staph infection
brought real-life Grant Morrison to death’s

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:27.670
doorstep — “I found that if I put the
character through a situation where he’d

00:01:27.670 --> 00:01:32.369
been tortured; where his lungs had bust and
he was being held in captivity; subjected

00:01:32.369 --> 00:01:33.990
to all these awful things.

00:01:33.990 --> 00:01:38.899
Three months later, I’m in hospital, two
bust lungs, dying of blood poisoning, and

00:01:38.899 --> 00:01:42.880
facing exactly the same shamanic trial I put
my character through.”

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:47.049
— then, Christ appeared to him, after which
he was healed and gained the superpower to

00:01:47.049 --> 00:01:49.049
cure cancer in cats.

00:01:49.049 --> 00:01:53.310
But all of that pales in comparison to his
experience in Kathmandu, — “I’d been

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:57.070
abducted by aliens in Kathmandu in 1994.”

00:01:57.070 --> 00:02:02.079
— where he was abducted by “rippling,
dripping blobs of pure holographic meta-material

00:02:02.079 --> 00:02:06.369
angels” and brought to a “mega-medium
in which entire universes are suspended”

00:02:06.369 --> 00:02:07.890
and shown the nature of reality.

00:02:07.890 --> 00:02:08.890
Allegedly.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:14.170
It was like a burrito of religious revelation
and alien abduction all rolled into one.

00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:18.409
Whether he actually ascended to a heavenly
hyperspace or was just trippin’ balls is

00:02:18.409 --> 00:02:19.409
beside the point.

00:02:19.409 --> 00:02:23.560
There’s a surprising amount of sober-minded
philosophy and physics that seem to agree

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:27.890
with certain aspects of Morrison’s Kathmandu
experience, including why he believes that

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:32.689
the universe is essentially just a giant comic
book and how he’s the midwife to an incredible

00:02:32.689 --> 00:02:35.959
future wherein we become god.

00:02:35.959 --> 00:02:39.599
Welcome to this Wisecrack Edition on the Philosophy
of Grant Morrison.

00:02:39.599 --> 00:02:44.120
Spoilers ahead, and if you wanna dive even
deeper into the mind of this mad genius, join

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:49.050
us over at wisecrackplus.com for an hour-long
podcast interview with the creators of this

00:02:49.050 --> 00:02:50.050
video.

00:02:50.050 --> 00:02:54.689
To be clear, Morrison is not suggesting we
could ever become the capital-G God described

00:02:54.689 --> 00:03:00.010
in the Bible, Torah, or the Koran, you know,
a single almighty creator of all things.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.299
When comic writers use the word “god,”
it’s more often with a lowercase “g,”

00:03:04.299 --> 00:03:08.849
like norse-deity-turned Marvel hero Thor or
other immensely powerful beings.

00:03:08.849 --> 00:03:14.189
With this logic, Morrison regards the Justice
League as the pantheon of American Mythology,

00:03:14.189 --> 00:03:18.970
and deliberately structured his roster in
JLA to parallel their Olympian counterparts.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:23.750
Now, we want to be very specific in saying
he doesn’t merely mean humans will become

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:25.980
super-powered posthumans one day.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.970
He means something much more.

00:03:27.970 --> 00:03:32.680
It’s Captain Atom who uh... “inspired”
Moore’s Dr. Manhattan that gives us our

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:35.620
first hint as to what Morrison means by “God.”

00:03:35.620 --> 00:03:40.620
Both Captain Atom and Dr. Manhattan achieve
god status because of their view of time,

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:43.739
they experience every event in their lives
simultaneously.

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:48.380
It’s not that they have perfect recall of
the past and total knowledge of the future.

00:03:48.380 --> 00:03:50.980
For them, there’s no past or future.

00:03:50.980 --> 00:03:54.629
Instead, every single moment is equally, eternally
real.

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:59.329
Picture a film reel: as it rolls forward at
twenty-four frames per second, the light projects

00:03:59.329 --> 00:04:02.049
one image on the screen at any given moment.

00:04:02.049 --> 00:04:07.060
In this analogy, the screen is reality; and
only that single frame is “real.”

00:04:07.060 --> 00:04:11.829
A frame pops into existence as the projector
illuminates it and pops out of existence as

00:04:11.829 --> 00:04:13.629
we move onto the next frame.

00:04:13.629 --> 00:04:15.150
This is what philosopher J.M.E.

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:17.480
McTaggart calls the A-Theory of Time.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:20.620
It’s how we, as humans, perceive time.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:25.380
McTaggart’s B-Theory, on the other hand,
is that events do not have objective tense.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:29.851
So, imagine if we were to illuminate each
frame of the film reel all at once, so that

00:04:29.851 --> 00:04:33.690
we could simultaneously gaze upon every single
moment in the movie.

00:04:33.690 --> 00:04:38.090
The main school of thought among B-Theorists
is Eternalism, which claims every moment in

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:42.360
time is eternal, from the Big Bang to the
Heat Death of the Universe.

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.270
Nothing ever pops in or out of existence.

00:04:45.270 --> 00:04:48.450
Rather, events exist alongside one another.

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:52.390
The shattering of this illusion is a frequent
trope in Morrison's writings.

00:04:52.390 --> 00:04:57.130
In The Invisibles, Lord Fanny’s initiation
into magic is accomplished at the age of eleven

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:01.391
when the remainder of Fanny’s life - getting
raped and beaten in Rio, drugged out in a

00:05:01.391 --> 00:05:06.790
drag club in London, even eventually dying
- is experienced as if it already happened.

00:05:06.790 --> 00:05:11.310
The Aztec goddess Tlazolteotl reveals to Fanny:
“Time is not a river.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:16.130
Time is more like a bubble but is to a bubble
what a bubble is to a circle drawn on the

00:05:16.130 --> 00:05:17.130
ground…”

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:20.340
So, Morrison philosophizes about the nature
of time in his stories.

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:21.340
Cool, right?

00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:22.360
Well, he takes it further.

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:23.390
Way further.

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:28.430
To Morrison, a slightly more literal version
of this B-theory of time lies in the very

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:30.300
medium of the comic book.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:35.350
On any given page, if were we to ask the heroes,
they’d tell us that page and that panel

00:05:35.350 --> 00:05:38.470
was the present moment, so far as they could
tell.

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:43.880
But as the reader, we see that all the panels,
all the pages, all the printed issues, are

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.340
all equally now.

00:05:46.340 --> 00:05:50.370
Their time is an illusion, but one to which
we’re not subjected.

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:54.960
Even the two-dimensional flat piece of paper
on which comics are drawn may be an apt analog

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:56.880
for the structure of spacetime.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:05:58.460
Per Morrison: “Our universe…

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:04.300
may even all be a hologram, projected onto
a flat, mega-membrane, which is, in turn,

00:06:04.300 --> 00:06:09.370
embedded, along with many others like it,
within a higher dimensional space[.]” “Oh,

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:10.370
gnarly!”

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:14.460
He's referring, in his own way, to the Holographic
Principle.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:18.530
Per Stephen Hawking, “Information associated
with all phenomena in the three-dimensional

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:23.750
world can be stored on a two-dimensional boundary,
like a holographic image.

00:06:23.750 --> 00:06:28.580
In a certain sense the world would be two-dimensional,”
like the pages of a comic book.

00:06:28.580 --> 00:06:32.630
So far, everything Morrison’s said seems
like a reasonable conclusion to draw from

00:06:32.630 --> 00:06:37.210
mainstream theoretical physics, and if all
he had to say was that, just as a film reel

00:06:37.210 --> 00:06:42.330
is a fitting analogy for A-Theory, so, too,
is a comic book an apt analogy for the B-Theory

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:45.830
of Time, his contribution would be entirely
warranted.

00:06:45.830 --> 00:06:50.180
But then “The Kathmandu Experience” went
down, and things got weird.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:55.120
Now, although his more out-there ideas don’t
necessarily hold up to scientific scrutiny,

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:58.810
they’re still worth exploring because they
take comic books and superhero stories and

00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:01.300
turn them into a compelling worldview.

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:05.890
Whether warranted or not, many look to figures
like Superman and Captain America for moral

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:07.150
inspirational.

00:07:07.150 --> 00:07:11.949
Morrison offers some justification for emulating
fictional heroes, treating them as gods, and

00:07:11.949 --> 00:07:17.060
even goes so far as to say that, “The idea
of Superman is every bit as real as the idea

00:07:17.060 --> 00:07:22.080
of God,” and suggests we’re even more
super, even more godlike.

00:07:22.080 --> 00:07:27.140
It’s for this reason that Morrison depicts
philosopher Giovanni Pico della Mirandola

00:07:27.140 --> 00:07:32.280
in the pages of All-Star Superman delivering
his famous “Oration on the Dignity of Man.”

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:37.080
As he later said in his autobiography, “[Pico’s]
Oratorio is without a doubt urging us to go

00:07:37.080 --> 00:07:41.180
far beyond the human, into the realm of angels
and gods.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:46.150
It asks us to accept the superhuman as an
undeniable fact of our nature, and the goal

00:07:46.150 --> 00:07:48.640
of our future evolution as a people…

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:53.440
Although his metaphors are Biblical, suggesting
Cherubs and Seraphs and Thrones as our role

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.670
models and intermediaries on the road to ‘God’
or ‘cosmic consciousness,’ we can just

00:07:57.670 --> 00:07:59.940
as easily call them superheroes.”

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:04.860
So, what happened at Kathmandu that made Morrison
believe that the universe really is a comic

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:07.070
book, and that humanity really is a superhero?

00:08:07.070 --> 00:08:12.690
Well, he claims to have stepped outside the
multiverse and seen reality from the outside:

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:18.180
“The universe – the entire space-time
continuum, from big bang to heat death, no

00:08:18.180 --> 00:08:22.950
less – was not a linear stream of events
with beginning, middle, and end.

00:08:22.950 --> 00:08:25.490
That was only how it felt from the inside…

00:08:25.490 --> 00:08:32.690
All life on Earth was one thing, a single,
weird, anemone-like mega-Hydra with its single-celled

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:39.080
immortal root in the Precambrian tides…
a slowly complexifying, increasingly self-aware

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:40.080
super-organism…

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.550
‘It’ was all of us, all life as seen from
the perspective of a higher dimension.”

00:08:45.550 --> 00:08:46.550
“Whoooa.”

00:08:46.550 --> 00:08:51.320
Whereas Eternalism is content to conclude
that spacetime is a timeless, four-dimensional

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:56.821
object, Morrison goes beyond Eternalism by
claiming that by the same principle, every

00:08:56.821 --> 00:09:03.810
human being is actually a mere cell in a single
super-organism that is Humanity.

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:09.270
And therefore, if each one of us is part of
a larger whole, then altruism and beneficence

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:12.810
and charity are in our own self-interest.

00:09:12.810 --> 00:09:17.300
The simplistic divide of Right and Wrong,
and Good and Evil, in superhero comics suddenly

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:22.440
seems less silly because our morals aren’t
arbitrary social constructs; they’re the

00:09:22.440 --> 00:09:27.750
genetic programing instructing this superorganism
not to destroy itself.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:32.470
Indeed, this view, as a basis for ethics,
is the very conclusion Lex Luthor reaches

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:37.630
when he gains Superman’s powers and, upon
seeing with his super-senses all the fundamental

00:09:37.630 --> 00:09:42.760
forces and how they’re interconnected, he
begins to weep: “This is how he sees all

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:44.130
the time, every day.

00:09:44.130 --> 00:09:47.450
Like, it’s all just us, in here, together.

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:48.840
And we’re all we’ve got.”

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:53.450
Okay, so if Humanity as a whole is like a
superhero comic book, we should therefore

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:55.630
behave like these costumed do-gooders.

00:09:55.630 --> 00:10:00.300
It’s a compelling case, but if you think
that’s weird, he also saw 5th dimensional

00:10:00.300 --> 00:10:06.151
aliens, or what he called “mercurial hypersprites”:
“Five-dimensional intelligences could, as

00:10:06.151 --> 00:10:11.280
a condition of their geometrically elevated
position, get into our skulls quite easily,

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:14.230
and we could expect their voices to come from
inside.

00:10:14.230 --> 00:10:19.990
They, in turn, could hear our thoughts as
easily as we can read Batman’s private inner

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:22.260
monologues on a 2-D page.”

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:26.090
This is illustrated in Multiversity: Pax Americana
#1.

00:10:26.090 --> 00:10:30.990
While reading a comic book, Captain Atom muses
to two scientists: “The story’s linear,

00:10:30.990 --> 00:10:34.530
but I can flip through the pages in any order,
any direction.

00:10:34.530 --> 00:10:36.590
Forward in time to the conclusion.

00:10:36.590 --> 00:10:38.130
Back to the opening scene.

00:10:38.130 --> 00:10:43.360
The characters remain unaware of my scrutiny,
but their thoughts are transparent, weightless

00:10:43.360 --> 00:10:44.690
in little clouds.

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:47.610
This is how a 2-dimensional continuum looks
to you.”

00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:52.550
Then, staring straight ahead he says, “Imagine
how your 3-D world appears to me.”

00:10:52.550 --> 00:10:57.910
Atom’s fourth wall break brings us to another
major theme in Morrison’s works, metafictionality,

00:10:57.910 --> 00:11:02.740
which is the exploration of the relationship
between the fiction itself with the real world

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:04.730
of the writer and reader.

00:11:04.730 --> 00:11:09.220
Morrison first wrestled with metafictionality
in the final issue of his run on Animal-Man,

00:11:09.220 --> 00:11:12.440
in which he donned what he calls a “fiction-suit.”

00:11:12.440 --> 00:11:16.590
He inserts himself into the comic itself to
interact with the character.

00:11:16.590 --> 00:11:21.370
He notes: "I explained to my character how
the people who wrote his life needed drama

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:24.370
and shock and violence to make his story interesting.

00:11:24.370 --> 00:11:28.970
The implication was that our own lives might
be ‘written’ to entertain or to instruct

00:11:28.970 --> 00:11:33.440
an audience in a perpendicular direction we
could never point to, interacting with us

00:11:33.440 --> 00:11:37.930
in ways we could scarcely understand but that
could be divined in the relationship of the

00:11:37.930 --> 00:11:41.390
comic world to the world of the creator and
audience.”

00:11:41.390 --> 00:11:46.360
This is the crux that connects Morrison’s
metafiction to his musings on spacetime: if

00:11:46.360 --> 00:11:51.800
comic books reflect the nature of our universe,
can we say that the physical comic-like universe

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:54.660
reflects the nature of some higher reality?

00:11:54.660 --> 00:11:57.670
Who are our cosmic “comic” creators?

00:11:57.670 --> 00:12:02.180
Who writes the plot lines of history, pencils
the particle interactions, letters the thought

00:12:02.180 --> 00:12:03.940
balloons of intelligent minds?

00:12:03.940 --> 00:12:08.600
Morrison’s answer is the fifth dimensional
aliens that we can't grasp with our feeble

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:13.029
three-dimensional minds, but he admits the
particulars are less important than the implications

00:12:13.029 --> 00:12:15.970
that follow from there being intelligent designers.

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:20.200
If the analogy of our universe being like
a comic book extends so far as to suggest

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:24.720
that it has something like a writer, then
the mere fact that it is a “written” work

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.650
implies a purposefulness to our existence.

00:12:27.650 --> 00:12:32.000
Now, of course, being created doesn’t necessarily
impart dignity.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:36.160
We might be like Morrison’s Animal Man,
with all our suffering for no greater purpose

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:39.160
than to provide drama in a piece of cheap
entertainment.

00:12:39.160 --> 00:12:40.790
We might be made by a hack.

00:12:40.790 --> 00:12:43.240
Our whole purpose might be to pass butter.

00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:48.130
But contra his younger self when writing Animal
Man, the more mature Morrison became more

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:49.130
optimistic.

00:12:49.130 --> 00:12:54.370
In Flex Mentallo: Man of Muscle Mystery, Morrison
once again used metafictionality as a means

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:57.339
of exploring questions about our own purpose.

00:12:57.339 --> 00:13:01.980
Just as Morrison sees reality as having a
three-tiered structure, with his Fifth Dimensional

00:13:01.980 --> 00:13:07.900
imps at the top, the 2D comic book characters
at the bottom, and us in the middle, so too

00:13:07.900 --> 00:13:11.860
is the story of Flex Mentallo set across three
levels of reality.

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:17.279
There’s the cartoon world of Flex Mentallo,
the “realistic” world of Flex’s writer,

00:13:17.279 --> 00:13:23.200
Wallace Sage (an avatar of Morrison himself),
and yet a higher reality inhabited by superheroes

00:13:23.200 --> 00:13:28.350
more real than their writers’- or the 5th
dimensional aliens.

00:13:28.350 --> 00:13:33.420
In the story, it's revealed that superheroes,
instead of being merely a product of the imagination

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:38.960
of writers like Wally, were in fact the beings
that created Wally’s entire world, after

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:43.529
which they retreated into the imagination,
seeding our culture with superhero stories

00:13:43.529 --> 00:13:48.450
in anticipation of the day that mere mortals
are ready to become like them.

00:13:48.450 --> 00:13:52.450
As a result, humanity only needs to resolve
their uncertainty about whether superheroes

00:13:52.450 --> 00:13:56.110
are real or not and decide to believe them
into existence.

00:13:56.110 --> 00:14:00.279
Here, Morrison is suggesting a much more dignified
purpose to our existence.

00:14:00.279 --> 00:14:04.990
We’re meant to discover our fundamental
purpose in the pages of comic books and to

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:10.980
believe into existence the idea of the Superman,
to incarnate him in ourselves.

00:14:10.980 --> 00:14:15.300
For Morrison, the medium of comics and the
plot structure of superhero stories are so

00:14:15.300 --> 00:14:20.470
closely connected in our culture because both
are parts of the same pattern that explain

00:14:20.470 --> 00:14:22.490
everything else in existence.

00:14:22.490 --> 00:14:27.770
In recognizing this pattern, Morrison writes
his own autobiography Supergods in the style

00:14:27.770 --> 00:14:33.170
and structure of an archetypal superhero story,
including a secret origin in Glasgow, the

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:37.740
call to adventure atop Kathmandu, and a death
and rebirth in which he brings the hidden

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:41.850
knowledge he gained on his Hero’s Journey
to the comics community.

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:46.060
And while that hidden knowledge is parsed
out in piecemeal in his comics writing, it’s

00:14:46.060 --> 00:14:52.080
in Supergods where he’s most explicit about
just what makes humankind itself a “supergod.”

00:14:52.080 --> 00:14:57.000
Already, we can see Humankind as having all
the attributes of comic book superheroes or

00:14:57.000 --> 00:14:58.340
even pagan gods.

00:14:58.340 --> 00:15:03.270
Whereas Mercury carried messages at the speed
of thought to the Roman gods, we have instantaneous

00:15:03.270 --> 00:15:05.460
interconnectivity via the Internet.

00:15:05.460 --> 00:15:10.260
Whereas Heimdallr’s all-seeing eyes could
spy everywhere in all nine realms, our satellites

00:15:10.260 --> 00:15:12.800
can zoom in on any spot on earth.

00:15:12.800 --> 00:15:15.660
Like Superman, we’ve flown faster than a
speeding bullet.

00:15:15.660 --> 00:15:20.080
Humanity, when viewed as a whole, is already
a “supergod.”

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:24.990
Insofar as there is no future frames of the
film reel popping into existence, we are not

00:15:24.990 --> 00:15:29.140
“becoming” this supergod - we already
always are.

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:34.190
This epoch of human history is merely its
secret origin story as to how it gets its

00:15:34.190 --> 00:15:38.820
superhuman powers, but all of its ongoing
adventures and never-ending battles to come

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:44.450
are all already written, published, printed,
and on the shelves of some higher dimensional

00:15:44.450 --> 00:15:45.640
comic shop.

00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:50.339
If mankind is this superorganism with all
these stupendous superpowers who’s supposed

00:15:50.339 --> 00:15:54.940
to act according to the simple morality of
a superhero, whose whole history is structured

00:15:54.940 --> 00:15:59.820
like a superhero story, and who’s living
in a cosmos that’s constructed like a comic

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:05.780
book, then Morrison’s obvious conclusion
is this: mankind’s purpose is to be a superhero.

00:16:05.780 --> 00:16:10.670
It’s for this reason that Morrison calls
the Sun-god from Smallville “our greatest-ever

00:16:10.670 --> 00:16:12.860
idea as the human species.”

00:16:12.860 --> 00:16:17.270
Not because Superman was the prototype on
which all other superheroes would be based,

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:22.770
because he is the template for which the supergod
of humanity must become, all-powerful and

00:16:22.770 --> 00:16:25.580
all-good, all at the same time.

00:16:25.580 --> 00:16:30.110
This is the summation of Morrison’s philosophy,
articulated best by Jor-El to his one and

00:16:30.110 --> 00:16:35.550
only son regarding the essential truth about
man and Superman, and not the mankind within

00:16:35.550 --> 00:16:40.850
the comics, but about the mankind reading
comics: "You have given them an ideal to aspire

00:16:40.850 --> 00:16:43.470
to, embodied their highest aspirations.

00:16:43.470 --> 00:16:49.270
They will race, and stumble, and fall, and
crawl, and curse, and finally ... they will

00:16:49.270 --> 00:17:03.860
join you 

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:38.129
in 

00:17:38.129 --> 00:17:41.940
the sun, Kal-el."

